            <ArticleTitle>[Advances in the study of A2B adenosine receptor antagonists].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>241-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A2B adenosine receptor is involved in the control of mast cell degranulation, interleukin-8 synthesis and cell growth. A2B adenosine receptor antagonists may serve as novel drugs for asthma, Alzheimer' s disease, cystic fibrosis and type-II diabetes. Therefore, seeking for the highly selective A2B adenosine receptor antagonists has been one of great interest. The molecular basis, structure-activity relationship of selective A2B adenosine receptor antagonists and their interactions with A2B adenosine receptor were reviewed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
